---
title: Interceptors
description: The four pipeline stages — pre, post, exception and final — how they compose, and the three arities each one comes in.
---

Interceptors are how cross-cutting concerns attach to a dispatch without handlers knowing
about them. They are discovered and registered exactly like handlers, and they run as part of
the same pre-built pipeline plan.

There are **four stages**:

```
pre*  →  main handler  →  post*        →  final*
                       ↘  exception*  ↗
```

- **pre** runs before the handler and may **rewrite the message**.
- **post** runs after a successful handler and may **rewrite the result**.
- **exception** runs when the handler or an interceptor threw, and may **supply a result**.
- **final** always runs last, on success and on failure alike.

Within a stage, order is `[Weight]`-descending, direct registrations before covariant ones.
See [Groups and weights](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/groups-and-weights).

## Pre-interceptors

A pre-interceptor returns the message that the next stage will see. Returning the input
unchanged is the pass-through case:

```csharp
public sealed class LogCommands : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Three arities are available, trading breadth for type safety:

| Interface | `HandleAsync` signature | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `ICommandPreInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, IExecutionContext)` | Applies to every command |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<object>(TCommand, IExecutionContext)` | Applies to one command type or hierarchy |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand, TModified>` | `ValueTask<TModified>(TCommand, IExecutionContext)` | You actually rewrite the message and want it typed |

In the two-parameter form, `TModified` must derive from `TCommand`. It exists for rewriting,
so if you are only observing, use the one-parameter form:

```csharp
public sealed class NormalizeName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct, CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

## Post-interceptors

A post-interceptor receives the result and returns the result the caller will get:

```csharp
public sealed class StampProduct : ICommandPostInterceptor<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, Guid result, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _audit.Record(command.Name, result);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

| Interface | `HandleAsync` signature |
|---|---|
| `ICommandPostInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, object, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<object>(TCommand, object, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>` | `ValueTask<TResult>(TCommand, TResult, IExecutionContext)` |

Post-interceptors do **not** run when the pipeline threw. That is what the exception stage is
for.

## Exception interceptors

```csharp
public sealed class SwallowNotFound : ICommandExceptionInterceptor<GetOrCreate, Guid>
{
    public ValueTask<Guid?> HandleAsync(
        GetOrCreate command, Guid? result, Exception exception, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (exception is not KeyNotFoundException)
            throw exception;                 // not ours — let it propagate

        return ValueTask.FromResult<Guid?>(Guid.Empty);
    }
}
```

The interceptor receives whatever result had been produced so far (often `null`) and the
exception. Returning a value satisfies the caller; rethrowing lets the exception propagate.

## Final interceptors

Final interceptors always run — success, failure, or abort — which makes them the right place
for cleanup, metrics and audit trails. They cannot change anything:

```csharp
public sealed class RecordDuration : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        ICommand command, object? result, Exception? exception, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (context.TryGet<long>("started", out var started))
            _metrics.Record(command.GetType().Name, Stopwatch.GetElapsedTime(started), exception);

        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

Note the signature: both `result` and `exception` are nullable, because exactly one of them
is meaningful on any given run.

## Coverage across modules

Every module offers all four stages, but not every arity exists everywhere — events have no
typed result, so their post and exception interceptors receive a `ValueTask` rather than a
result value.

| Stage | Commands | Queries | Events |
|---|---|---|---|
| Pre | `ICommandPreInterceptor` `<TCommand>` `<TCommand, TModified>` | `IQueryPreInterceptor` `<TQuery>` `<TQuery, TModified>` | `IEventPreInterceptor` `<TEvent>` `<TEvent, TModified>` |
| Post | `ICommandPostInterceptor` `<TCommand>` `<TCommand, TResult>` | `IQueryPostInterceptor` `<TQuery>` `<TQuery, TResult>` | `IEventPostInterceptor` `<TEvent>` |
| Exception | `ICommandExceptionInterceptor` `<TCommand>` `<TCommand, TResult>` | `IQueryExceptionInterceptor` `<TQuery, TResult>` | `IEventExceptionInterceptor` `<TEvent>` |
| Final | `ICommandFinalInterceptor` `<TCommand>` `<TCommand, TResult>` | `IQueryFinalInterceptor` `<TQuery, TResult>` | `IEventFinalInterceptor` `<TEvent>` |

The [API reference](/ergosfare.docs/api) carries the exact signature for each one.

## Covariant matching

An interceptor registered against a base type or interface applies to **every assignable
message**. This is the main reason to prefer a marker interface over a base class:

```csharp
public interface IAuditedCommand : ICommand;

public sealed class AuditTrail : ICommandPreInterceptor<IAuditedCommand>
{
    public ValueTask<IAuditedCommand> HandleAsync(IAuditedCommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _audit.Log(command);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(command);
    }
}
```

Every command implementing `IAuditedCommand` now goes through `AuditTrail`, with no
registration per command.

Within a stage, interceptors registered for the message type itself run **before** covariantly
matched ones. See [Polymorphism](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/polymorphism).

## Opting a message out

`[ExcludeFromPipeline]` on a *message* removes it from covariantly matched interceptors —
useful for a high-volume message you do not want the blanket logging interceptor to touch:

```csharp
[ExcludeFromPipeline]
public sealed record Heartbeat : ICommand;

[ExcludeFromPipeline("logging")]     // or just one group
public sealed record Tick : ICommand;
```

Interceptors registered for the message type *itself* always run — they were written for that
message deliberately. Main handlers are never affected.

## What interceptors are not

They are not handlers. An interceptor does not perform the operation; it observes, enriches,
short-circuits or cleans up around it. If a piece of logic is the point of the message, it
belongs in the handler.

Next: [Polymorphism](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/polymorphism).
